<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vosges</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Vosges Mountains</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vosges are a range of low mountains in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, running parallel to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> on the western side of the Upper Rhine plain and separating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>. Their highest summit, the Grand Ballon, reaches 1,424 m. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the range lay within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, with abbeys, small principalities and imperial towns on its slopes; a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> also bears its name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vosges.jpg" alt="Vosges" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tangopaso · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Houses_near_Bussang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Vosges run north-north-east from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfort-gap">Burgundian Gate</a> to the Börrstadt Basin and form the western boundary of the Upper Rhine Plain. Together with the Palatine Forest on the German side of the border they make a single low mountain range of around 8,000 square kilometres. The range is divided from south to north into the Higher Vosges, whose rounded summits are called ballons in French, the sandstone Middle Vosges and the Lower Vosges, a sandstone plateau between 300 and 560 metres high. After the Grand Ballon come the Storkenkopf at 1,366 metres and the Hohneck at 1,364 metres.</p><p>The Vosges and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> were both formed by uplift beside the Rhine Graben, and they are alike in geology and in their forested lower slopes. Westerly winds bring heavy rain and snow to the western slope, while the Alsatian plain beneath the range is much drier, and vineyards on the eastern slope climb to about 400 metres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> the massif was called Vosago mons, Vosego silva or Vogesus mons, a name extended to the vast woods of the region; German speakers later called it Vogesen or Wasgenwald. Forests were cleared over the centuries for farming and livestock and for early industry, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a> works, glassworks and water mills. In the mining valley of the Lièpvrette, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> miners settled. Castles, mostly ruined, stand on the Alsatian slopes, and at Sainte Odile survives the Mur Païen, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dry-stone-construction">dry-stone</a> wall that archaeologists are divided on whether the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built or whether it is older.</p><p>From 1871 to 1918 the range formed the main border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, running from the Ballon d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> to Mont Donon. It saw almost continuous fighting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. In October 1944 the U.S. 442nd Regiment, composed of second-generation Japanese Americans, fought a fierce battle on the mountain to rescue the cut-off 1st Battalion of the 36th Infantry, the Lost Battalion, and suffered 800 casualties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The French department of Vosges is named after the range. Two nature parks lie within it, the Northern Vosges Regional Nature Park, established in 1976, and the Ballons des Vosges Nature Park, established in 1989. The Northern Vosges park and the Palatinate Forest park across the border form a cross-border <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> biosphere reserve.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vosges” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most people were peasants growing grain and vines in the Moselle and Meuse valleys, with pasture and forest in the <b>Vosges</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Coins at Sarrebourg show its temple in use until near the century’s end, and Cumont thought the cult may have survived in remote cantons of the Alps and <b>Vosges</b> into the 5th century.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>…and on 16 June 1916 he sang it at the inn of mother Misquette at Étival-Clairefontaine behind the front in the <b>Vosges</b>, where it was a great success; from there it spread along the front, carried by the gunners and by the comic soldier-singers popular in the war.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-madelon">La Madelon</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Franco-Dutch War, the French army under Marshal Turenne made a surprise winter march through the <b>Vosges</b> and defeated the imperial and Brandenburg forces near Colmar.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-5">5 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Montbéliard guarded the Belfort Gap between the Jura and the <b>Vosges</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-montbeliard">County of Montbéliard</a></li><li><blockquote>Lotharingia was the kingdom created for Lothair II in 855, stretching from Frisia to the <b>Vosges</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-lotharingia">Duchy of Lotharingia</a></li></ul></section>
